Prepared for: Assistant Director of Nursing, Santé of MesaPrepared by: CNA project sponsor / facility review teamDate: August 4, 2026Status: Draft for Nursing, Privacy/Security, Infection Prevention, Therapy, Dietary/SLP, Health Information Management, and Legal/Compliance review

Important: This is a proposed workflow and design standard—not a medical order, legal opinion, approved facility policy, or substitute for the EHR, Kardex, assignment record, physician orders, or person-centered care plan. No real resident information should be entered into the current public demonstration app.

Executive recommendation

Approve a limited paper-form pilot after interdisciplinary review. Continue testing the web app with synthetic data only until Santé's Privacy/Security Officer and IT team approve a production architecture.

The current open-source prototype is useful as a design demonstration, but it is not ready for protected health information (PHI). It has no authentication, role-based access, audit trail, approved retention control, conflict handling, or server-side security; it stores resident details in browser localStorage and exports plaintext JSON. “Free software” does not make a workflow HIPAA-compliant. Production use requires facility governance, secure managed devices, an approved vendor/service arrangement, risk analysis, policies, training, and—when a cloud service provider maintains ePHI—an appropriate business associate agreement (BAA).

Governing design principles

Care-plan derived: Every resident-specific instruction must come from the current order, assignment, or person-centered plan of care. CMS requires services to be delivered by qualified persons in accordance with the written care plan (42 CFR §483.21; 42 CFR §483.25).

Handoff aid, not source of truth: If the sheet and EHR disagree, stop, protect the resident, and confirm with the licensed nurse/current order. Never “split the difference.”

Minimum necessary: Include only information needed for the CNA's assigned care and handoff. HHS requires covered entities to evaluate and limit unnecessary access or disclosure of PHI (HHS Minimum Necessary guidance).

Objective and actionable: Record the assistance, equipment, timing, precautions, resident preferences, observed change, and escalation—not stigmatizing labels or unsupported clinical conclusions.

Closed-loop handoff: The outgoing CNA identifies current status, changes, completed/outstanding tasks, and what to watch for; the incoming CNA reads back high-risk items and accepts the assignment. AHRQ recommends structured communication whenever responsibility changes, including shift changes and short breaks (AHRQ long-term-care handoff guidance).

One workflow, two surfaces: Paper and digital views must use the same facility-approved terms, data definitions, task times, escalation rules, and revision date.

Section 1 — Essential CNA Shift Data Matrix

There is no universal federal list of “mandatory brain-sheet fields.” The facility determines the form from applicable law, current orders, the resident's assessment and person-centered care plan, staff scope/competency, and local policy. The following is a recommended minimum operational dataset for a 12-resident SNF/Rehab assignment.

1.1 Assignment and sheet-control fields

Field

Requirement

Design rule

Facility, unit/hall, assignment

Always

Use facility-approved names; avoid defaulting to a real facility in a public template.

