CONFIDENTIAL PHI — KEEP SECURE — DISPOSE PER FACILITY POLICY.

1.2 Resident-level clinical and ADL matrix

Domain

Minimum field set

Allowed examples / format

Safety and scope guardrail

Identification

Room/bed; facility-approved resident identifier; preferred name

104-A · J. Smith · prefers “Jack”

Room number alone is not a bedside identity check. Exclude DOB, SSN, insurance numbers, and full MRN unless specifically approved as necessary.

Change flag

New admission/return; new or changed care-plan item; acute concern; verification time

NEW TRANSFER PLAN—verified 14:10

A paper flag never authorizes care. Confirm changes with the licensed nurse/current source.

Code-status alert

Facility-approved exact status, only if leadership determines it is necessary for CNA handoff

FULL CODE; DNR—VERIFY EHR

Optional pending policy. A POLST is an orders form, not one generic status. Never infer status, and never use the brain sheet as the emergency source of truth. Avoid FC; it is commonly confused with Foley catheter.

Diet / food texture

Exact current diet order; IDDSI food level/name if the facility uses IDDSI

IDDSI 6 Soft & Bite-Sized

Do not translate or “simplify” an order. “Mechanical soft” can be ambiguous. IDDSI uses drink Levels 0–4 and food Levels 3–7 (IDDSI Framework).

Liquid consistency

Exact ordered liquid level/name; permitted exceptions if ordered

IDDSI 2 Mildly Thick

Do not rely only on legacy terms such as nectar/honey. Match Dietary/SLP terminology exactly.

Meal safety and assistance

Independence/supervision/assist/total feed; positioning; adaptive equipment; aspiration precautions; meal location

1:1 feed · upright 90° · small sips

Only include active care-plan instructions. Report coughing, choking, wet voice, pocketing, reduced alertness, or change per policy; do not independently change texture.

Hydration

Fluid restriction or encouragement plan; amount assigned to shift; strict I&O task

FR 1500 mL/day · shift allotment ___ mL; Strict I&O

A daily limit without the shift allotment creates error risk. Licensed nurse/dietary plan determines allocation and escalation.

Bed mobility

Independent/supervision/1-person/2-person; device; side preference if care planned

2PA boost · draw sheet

Separate bed mobility from transfer and ambulation; one combined “assist level” is often unsafe.

Transfer

Assist level; transfer method; exact equipment; sling type/size if applicable

2PA full mechanical lift · size M sling

Use generic equipment terms where possible, not only brand names. Staff must be trained and follow the current transfer plan.

Ambulation

Assist level; device; weight-bearing status; distance or destination limits

1PA + gait belt + FWW · WBAT

Do not infer weight-bearing status. Report inability or a meaningful change before continuing.

Toileting / continence

Toileting schedule; continence pattern; assistance/equipment; brief size if operationally useful

Toilet q2h while awake · 1PA commode · brief L

Brief size is a supply field, not a complete continence plan. Preserve privacy and resident choice.

Urinary device

Device present; assigned observe/empty/measure task; securement/location checks

Foley—empty/measure q shift

CNA care is limited to trained, assigned tasks. Report obstruction, leakage, pain, blood, no output, or change; do not perform unassigned sterile procedures.

Ostomy

Pouch present; assigned observe/empty/measure task; supply location if needed

Ostomy—observe/empty/report

Avoid the vague phrase “ostomy care.” State only the exact delegated task for which competency and policy exist.

Bowel tracking

Last known BM date/shift if approved; bowel-monitoring task; report threshold

BM this shift: time ___; No BM—notify per protocol

Do not use a single BM 1/2/3 checkbox; its meaning is unclear. Document required findings in the official record.

Bathing / hygiene

Shower/bed bath schedule and shift; assistance; resident preference; oral care/dentures

Shower EVE · 1PA · prefers female staff; dentures HS

If refused, preserve choice, offer approved alternatives, report, and document per policy. A checkbox should distinguish done/refused/not due.

Dressing / grooming

Setup/min/mod/max/total assist; cueing; affected limb/precaution

Mod assist · dress affected arm first

Use the current therapy/nursing plan; do not invent rehabilitation technique.

Sensory / communication

Glasses, hearing aids, communication board, interpreter or cueing needs

Hearing aids · face resident · one-step cues

Supports person-centered care and reduces behavior mislabeling. Keep only actionable information.

Repositioning / offloading

Individualized schedule; position limits/preferences; heel/offloading device; chair pressure relief

Turn 06/09/12 · float heels; no left side

Do not default everyone to Q2H. Repositioning must follow the individualized care plan; AHRQ states care planning must fit the patient's needs (AHRQ pressure-injury care planning).

Skin observation

Areas requiring routine observation; protective device; exact report-now trigger

Observe sacrum/heels during care · report redness/open area

CNA observes and reports. Wound assessment, staging, and treatment direction belong to licensed clinicians unless a specific permissible task is delegated.

Vitals / weight

Exact task, time/frequency, equipment, and report-to-nurse requirement

VS 14:00; weight before breakfast

Arizona identifies vital signs, height, and weight as basic CNA/LNA skills. CNAs report abnormal physical or mental changes (Arizona Board of Nursing CNA/LNA FAQ, pp. 7–8).

Blood glucose

Ordered time(s); completion/report field

BG AC dinner / HS

In Arizona, experienced CNAs/LNAs may perform blood-glucose testing only when agency policy exists and competency is documented; results must be reported to a licensed nurse (Arizona Board FAQ, p. 10). Use BG, not ambiguous BS.

Oxygen

Device, ordered setting as a verify/report cue, and safety instruction

NC—ordered 2 L/min · do not titrate · report mismatch

Arizona guidance says CNA/LNA may remove/apply an oxygen device during transfers but may not change oxygen settings or tanks (Arizona Board FAQ, p. 10).

Fall / accident interventions

Specific current interventions: transfer help, footwear, lighting, call light, rounding/toileting, low bed, mat, device placement

1PA to toilet · non-skid footwear · low bed · round hourly

“Fall risk” alone is not actionable. Position alarms do not replace supervision and should not be the primary/sole fall intervention (CMS Appendix PP, F689 guidance).

Isolation / PPE

Exact ordered Transmission-Based Precaution and task-relevant PPE/equipment handling cue

CONTACT—follow door sign · dedicated BP cuff

Infection Prevention/charge nurse controls placement and precaution decisions. CDC recommends Transmission-Based Precautions in addition to Standard Precautions when indicated (CDC Isolation Precautions). The room sign/current policy remains authoritative.

Cognition / behavior support

Baseline cognition/communication; cueing; known triggers; preferred approach; elopement/wandering plan; observed change

Baseline: one-step cues · approach from front · music calms

Replace labels such as “combative” with observable behavior, trigger, effective approach, and escalation. Never diagnose. Report acute change.

Refusal / choice

Task refused, alternative offered, safety concern, nurse notified

Shower declined—bed bath offered—RN notified 15:20

A refusal is not task completion. Respect resident rights and follow the approved escalation/documentation process.

Therapy / appointments

Time, transport/ADL preparation, equipment, weight-bearing or clothing need

PT 10:00 · dressed by 09:30 · shoes/FWW

Include only what the CNA must do. Discharge teaching and clinical interpretation are not CNA handoff tasks.

Shift focus / contingency

Top 1–3 priorities; outstanding task; what change requires immediate nurse notification

NEW weakness → stay with resident/call nurse; weight still due

Use brief, objective, time-linked instructions. Emergency response always follows facility policy.

Handoff closure

Completed tasks; outstanding tasks; concern escalated to whom/when; receiver initials/read-back

RN Lee notified 18:05 · incoming CNA read-back

The checklist does not replace charting or an incident report.

1.3 Information that should not appear by default

Full diagnosis and medication lists, psychotherapy details, SSN, insurance information, full DOB, or other nonessential identifiers.

Insulin doses, medication administration directions, oxygen titration instructions, wound staging, or independent clinical decision rules for CNAs.

Subjective labels such as “difficult,” “noncompliant,” “aggressive,” or “combative.” Use observable facts and an approved response.

Copied-forward instructions without an updated/verified time.

Any real resident data in source code, GitHub issues, screenshots, demo files, or public documentation.

