Section 2 — Print Ergonomics and Black-and-White Standards

2.1 Page geometry

For US Letter landscape, 0.30-inch margins leave approximately 10.4 × 7.9 inches. Treat the one-page limit as a constraint, not a dare.

Layout

Geometry

Appropriate use

Content limit

12-resident summary

4 columns × 3 rows

Stable assignment, concise care-plan cues

About 2.5 × 2.1 inches per resident after header/footer/gaps; no narrative paragraph

8-resident standard

4 columns × 2 rows

Typical skilled/Rehab assignment

About 2.5 × 3.0 inches per resident; supports more task boxes

4-resident high acuity

2 columns × 2 rows

Complex transfer, skin, I&O, or behavior-support needs

Larger notes and time-specific trackers

1-resident detail

One resident per page

Admission/return, special observation, or teaching aid

Must not be presented as the care plan itself

Hard rule: If approved content cannot remain legible at 100% print scale, use two pages or a lower resident-count layout. Do not solve overflow with 6-point text.

2.2 Visual specification

Print body text: 8.0 pt minimum, 8.5–9 pt preferred; resident name/room 10–11 pt bold.

Use a system sans-serif stack for print (Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif) so printing is not dependent on internet fonts.

Use white backgrounds, black text, and 0.75–1.25 pt black rules. Avoid gray fills, gradients, shadows, reversed white-on-black badges, and decorative icons.

Keep field labels short and consistently positioned. Use whitespace and borders—not shading—to group sections.

Reserve at least 0.20–0.25 inch vertical height for handwriting lines and 4–5 mm boxes for handwritten checks/initials.

Prevent card splitting with break-inside: avoid; test the entire page, including metadata, legend, confidentiality footer, and printer nonprintable area.

Repeat the sheet date/shift/unit and page number on every page.

Print a visible revision identifier, e.g. CNA-HO-01 Rev 2026-08-04.

Test on the actual facility printers at 100% scale, duplex on/off, Chrome/Edge print engines, and copier/scanner reproduction. A browser preview alone is not acceptance testing.

