3.2 Preferred architecture

Best option: EHR-integrated handoff view. Pull current care-plan instructions and assignment data from the approved system, display the effective time, and write required completion data back to the official record. This minimizes duplicate, stale data.

Second option: facility-managed Google Workspace or equivalent. Use only a Santé-managed tenant and accounts, only services covered by the organization's accepted BAA and approval, with least-privilege access, MFA, managed devices, audit logging, retention, sharing restrictions, and a documented risk analysis. HHS states that a cloud service provider maintaining ePHI requires an appropriate BAA and HIPAA compliance by both parties (HHS Cloud Computing guidance).

Do not use personal Google Drive, personal email, personal devices, consumer file-sharing links, USB drives, or general shared folders for PHI.

3.3 Google Sheets design

If Santé approves Google Workspace for this use:

Use a protected master template owned by a service/facility account, not an individual employee.

Separate tables into Resident Care Profile (read-only imported/current instructions), Shift Assignment, Task Events, Handoff, and Print View.

Use dropdowns/data validation for assist level, equipment, IDDSI levels, task status, isolation type, and refusal status.

Protect formulas, definitions, and care-profile cells; CNAs edit only assigned task/status fields.

Stamp every change with user, date/time, shift ID, resident ID, and revision.

Provide a generated/verified time and stale-data warning on the print view.

Use filtered views per unit/assignment; never rely on hidden rows as an access-control mechanism.

Prohibit public links and external sharing; regularly review memberships and audit events.

Define end-of-shift disposition: merge required facts into the EHR, then retain or securely delete the working sheet according to HIM policy.

Google Docs is acceptable for the approved proposal or blank template, but it is a poor live data store: free text is hard to validate, audit, filter, or reconcile. JSON import/export is file transfer—not Google Docs integration.

3.4 Minimum production security controls

Unique user authentication, role-based authorization, MFA, automatic screen lock, and immediate offboarding.

Encryption in transit and at rest within an approved managed platform; secure backups and tested recovery.

Immutable audit events for view, create, update, print/export, import, deletion, and administrative changes.

Server-side schema validation, size limits, allowlisted values, safe rendering, and malware/content checks for imported files.

Version/revision control with conflict detection; never silently overwrite a newer shift record.

Automatic shift expiration/locking and a written retention/destruction schedule.

Managed printers, immediate pickup, secure transport, and locked shred bins. HHS requires reasonable safeguards for PHI disposal and says paper PHI should not enter publicly accessible trash unless rendered unreadable and unreconstructable (HHS Disposal guidance).

Downtime procedure and read-only emergency access that does not depend on an internet connection.

No real PHI in analytics, browser crash reports, source maps, screenshots, GitHub issues, demo data, or developer logs.

