Section 4 — One-Page Executive Proposal for the ADON

Proposal: Standardized CNA Shift Handoff and Task Sheet

Decision requested: Approve a 30-day, one-unit paper pilot of a standardized CNA shift report sheet, while restricting the current web app to synthetic data until Privacy/Security and IT approve a production design.

Problem

Current informal brain sheets vary by individual CNA. High-risk instructions—transfer method, diet/liquid consistency, toileting, skin/offloading, isolation, and time-critical tasks—may be scattered, abbreviated differently, copied forward, or omitted. Handoff quality depends too heavily on memory.

Proposed solution

One facility-approved, black-and-white landscape form using current care-plan instructions.

Standard resident sequence: status/change, assistance needs, shift-focus tasks, escalation/outstanding items, and read-back.

12-resident summary, 8-resident standard, and 4-resident high-acuity versions with identical terminology.

Time-specific completion boxes for meals, I&O, BM, individualized repositioning, bathing, vitals/weight, and BG when assigned.

A secure digital version only after access, audit, BAA/vendor, retention, EHR reconciliation, and incident-response controls are approved.

Expected benefits

Safer care: Makes resident-specific transfer, diet, isolation, fall-prevention, skin/offloading, and escalation instructions visible at handoff.

Fewer omissions: Time-specific task boxes make completed, refused, not-due, and outstanding work distinguishable.

Faster handoff: A consistent sequence reduces searching and repeated explanation; receiver read-back confirms high-risk items.

Person-centered care: Preferred name, communication support, routine, cueing, and effective approaches travel with the assignment.

Low implementation cost: The template and code can be openly licensed after branding/legal review, but compliance operations and managed infrastructure are not cost-free.

Non-negotiable safeguards

The EHR/current care plan remains the source of truth; discrepancies go to the licensed nurse.

The sheet contains minimum necessary PHI, stays secured, and is disposed of per policy.

No personal cloud accounts, personal devices, public links, plaintext JSON sharing, or PHI in GitHub.

CNA tasks remain within Arizona scope, documented competency, delegation, job description, and facility policy.

No universal Q2H turning, blanket alarms, oxygen titration, diet reinterpretation, or subjective behavior labels.

Pilot and measures

Interdisciplinary team approves fields, abbreviations, escalation rules, and disposal/retention.

Train pilot staff with two synthetic handoff scenarios and competency/read-back checks.

Pilot on one unit for 30 days; conduct weekly five-sheet audits.

Compare baseline vs. pilot:

median handoff duration;

percentage of high-risk fields complete/current;

number of missing/ambiguous transfer and diet instructions;

percentage of time-specific tasks clearly resolved or handed off;

late corrections/duplicate documentation;

privacy incidents and unsecured sheets;

CNA/nurse usability rating.

Review falls and pressure injuries as lagging, multi-factor outcomes; do not claim the form alone caused a change.

Approval path

ADON/DON → Privacy/Security & HIM → Infection Prevention → Dietary/SLP → Therapy → IT → QAPI/pilot approval

